# CURSOR RULES — TYPESCRIPT

**This file is authoritative.**  
**Cursor MUST follow these rules exactly.**

---

## TypeScript Safety, Performance & Quality Standards

---

### PURPOSE & SCOPE

This document defines the **STRICT TypeScript rules** for this repository.

**Audience:**

- All developers
- Cursor / Antigravity / AI coding assistants

**Goals:**

- Maximum type safety
- Predictable runtime behavior
- High TypeScript compiler performance
- Zero unsafe shortcuts
- Production-grade TypeScript only

---

### ESLINT ENFORCEMENT REFERENCE

These rules are mechanically enforced by ESLint.

**Authoritative ESLint configuration:** `.eslintrc.js`

Cursor MUST generate code that:

- Passes ESLint without disabling rules
- Does NOT weaken or override ESLint behavior
- Does NOT introduce `eslint-disable` comments
- Does NOT rely on formatter-only fixes to bypass rules

**If ESLint reports an error or warning:**

- The code is considered invalid
- The root cause MUST be fixed
- The rule MUST NOT be silenced

> **ESLint is the execution engine.**  
> **This document is the law.**

---

## 0) GOLDEN RULE

**TypeScript exists to expose bugs early.**

If TypeScript reports an error:

- The design is wrong
- The data flow is unsafe
- Or the type definition is incomplete

**NEVER silence TypeScript.**  
**ALWAYS fix the root cause.**

---

## 1) NON-NEGOTIABLE RULES (HARD FAIL)

The following are **STRICTLY FORBIDDEN:**

- `any`
- `as any`
- `unknown as X`
- `@ts-ignore`
- `@ts-nocheck`
- Type assertions used to bypass errors

**Allowed ONLY for third-party library typing bugs:**

```typescript
// TODO: remove when library types are fixed
```

---

## 2) STRICT COMPILER EXPECTATIONS

All code MUST compile with:

- `strict: true`
- `noImplicitAny: true`
- `strictNullChecks: true`
- `noUncheckedIndexedAccess: true`

**Never weaken `tsconfig.json` locally or per file.**

---

## 3) CONTROLLED USE OF `extends` IN TYPES & INTERFACES

Inheritance in TypeScript types is **DISCOURAGED by default**, but **ALLOWED in limited, well-defined cases only**.

`extends` MUST NOT be used casually or for every type.

### 3.1 When `extends` IS NOT ALLOWED (Default Rule)

Do NOT use `extends` for:

- Domain models (User, Institute, Batch, Order, etc.)
- API response shapes
- Feature-level data types
- UI props and state
- Business logic entities

**Forbidden examples:**

```typescript
interface Admin extends User {}
type ApiResponse<T> extends BaseResponse {}
```

**Why this restriction exists:**

- Creates hidden coupling between types
- Slows TypeScript in large codebases
- Makes refactors risky
- Encourages deep and fragile hierarchies

### 3.2 When `extends` IS ALLOWED (Exceptions)

`extends` MAY be used **ONLY** when ALL of the following are true:

- The base type is:
  - Very small
  - Stable
  - Unlikely to change
- The relationship is:
  - Purely structural
  - Not domain/business-driven
- The extension depth is:
  - Single-level only
  - No chains (A → B → C)

**Allowed examples:**

```typescript
interface WithId {
  id: string;
}

interface User extends WithId {
  name: string;
}
```

```typescript
interface BaseProps {
  testId?: string;
}

interface ButtonProps extends BaseProps {
  label: string;
  onPress(): void;
}
```

### 3.3 Rules for Allowed `extends`

When using `extends`, you MUST follow these rules:

- Only 1 base type
- No multi-level inheritance
- No conditional logic in base types
- No generics in base types unless unavoidable
- Base types must not import feature/domain logic

**If any rule is violated → use composition instead.**

### 3.4 Preferred Alternative (Still Recommended)

Even when `extends` is allowed, **composition is preferred:**

```typescript
type WithId = {
  id: string;
};

type User = {
  id: string;
  name: string;
};
```

Use `extends` only when it clearly improves readability and reduces duplication without hiding logic.

### RULE SUMMARY

- `extends` is NOT forbidden
- `extends` is NOT the default
- `extends` is for small, stable, shared shapes only
- Composition remains the primary pattern

---

## 4) APPROVED ALTERNATIVES TO `extends`

### 4.1 Explicit Composition (Preferred)

```typescript
type User = {
  id: string;
  name: string;
};

type Admin = {
  id: string;
  name: string;
  permissions: string[];
};
```

### 4.2 Controlled Intersection Types

```typescript
type WithId = {
  id: string;
};

type WithTimestamps = {
  createdAt: string;
  updatedAt: string;
};

type Entity = WithId & WithTimestamps;
```

**Rules:**

- Max 2–3 intersections
- Must be shallow
- No recursive or nested intersections

---

## 5) FUNCTION TYPING RULES

All functions MUST have:

- Explicit parameter types
- Explicit return types

**BAD:**

```typescript
function save(data) {
  return api.post(data);
}
```

**GOOD:**

```typescript
function save(data: SavePayload): Promise<SaveResult> {
  return api.post(data);
}
```

---

## 6) ASYNC & PROMISE SAFETY

**Rules:**

- Never return `Promise<any>`
- Never ignore promises
- Always await or return

**BAD:**

```typescript
async function load() {}
```

**GOOD:**

```typescript
async function load(): Promise<void> {}
```

---

## 7) NULL & UNDEFINED SAFETY

Never assume a value exists.

**BAD:**

```typescript
user.name.toUpperCase();
```

**GOOD:**

```typescript
user?.name?.toUpperCase();
```

If a value MUST exist:

- Validate explicitly
- Throw or handle gracefully

---

## 8) TYPE NARROWING IS MANDATORY

Use:

- `typeof`
- `in`
- `instanceof`
- Custom type guards

**Example:**

```typescript
function isUser(value: unknown): value is User {
  return typeof value === 'object' && value !== null && 'id' in value;
}
```

---

## 9) UNION TYPES OVER ENUMS

Avoid enums.

**BAD:**

```typescript
enum Status {
  Active,
  Inactive,
}
```

**GOOD:**

```typescript
type Status = 'active' | 'inactive';
```

---

## 10) GENERICS — STRICT DISCIPLINE

**Rules:**

- Use generics ONLY when reused
- Never default generics to `any`
- Never constrain generics using `extends object`

**BAD:**

```typescript
type Box<T extends object> = { value: T };
```

**GOOD:**

```typescript
type Box<T> = { value: T };
```

---

## 11) API & DATA CONTRACT RULES

**Rules:**

- Every API response must be typed
- Never trust backend blindly
- Always model error states

**Example pattern:**

```typescript
type ApiResult<T> =
  | { success: true; data: T }
  | { success: false; message: string };
```

---

## 12) PERFORMANCE-AWARE TYPING

**Avoid:**

- Conditional types
- Recursive types
- Deep mapped types
- Over-generic helpers

**Prefer:**

- Flat explicit types
- Readable shapes
- Maintainability over cleverness

---

## 13) CURSOR / AI ENFORCEMENT RULES

**AI MUST:**

- Avoid `any`
- Avoid `extends`
- Prefer composition
- Explicitly type everything
- Mark uncertainty clearly

**AI MUST NOT:**

- Silence TypeScript
- Cast away errors
- Introduce inheritance chains
- Add complex types without justification

---

## 14) FINAL LAW

> **If a type is hard to read, it is wrong.**  
> **If TypeScript is slow, the types are too clever.**  
> **Simple types scale. Complex types break teams.**

